Looking at this problem, I need to identify what space results from closing the step functions under the BV norm.

## Setup and Key Definitions

Let $[a,b]$ be a bounded interval. The **BV norm** of a function $f$ of bounded variation is

$$\|f\|_{BV} = \|f\|_\infty + V(f),$$

where $V(f) = |Df|([a,b])$ is the total variation of the signed measure $Df$ (the distributional derivative of $f$). Equivalently, $V(f) = \sup \sum |f(x_i) - f(x_{i-1})|$ over all partitions.

A **step function** is piecewise constant with finitely many jumps; its distributional derivative is a finite sum of Dirac masses (a **purely atomic** measure).

## Proof

**Claim:** The closure of the step functions in the BV norm is the space of **saltus functions** (also called **jump functions**): BV functions whose distributional derivative is a purely atomic measure.

A saltus function has the form
$$f(x) = c + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n \,\mathbf{1}_{[x_n, b]}(x), \qquad \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} |a_n| < \infty,$$
so that $Df = \sum_{n} a_n \,\delta_{x_n}$ is purely atomic with finite total mass.

### Step 1: Every step function is a saltus function

A step function has finitely many jumps, so its derivative is a finite sum of Dirac masses — a special case of a purely atomic measure. ✓

### Step 2: Step functions are dense in the saltus functions (BV norm)

Given a saltus function $f(x) = c + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n \mathbf{1}_{[x_n,b]}(x)$, define the partial sums
$$f_N(x) = c + \sum_{n=1}^{N} a_n \,\mathbf{1}_{[x_n,b]}(x),$$
which are step functions. Then
$$\|f_N - f\|_{BV} = \|f_N - f\|_\infty + V(f_N - f) \leq \sum_{n>N}|a_n| + \sum_{n>N}|a_n| = 2\sum_{n>N}|a_n| \xrightarrow{N\to\infty} 0.$$
So every saltus function is a BV-norm limit of step functions. ✓

### Step 3: The saltus functions are closed in BV norm

Suppose $\{f_n\}$ are saltus functions with $\|f_n - f\|_{BV} \to 0$. Then $V(f_n - f) = |Df_n - Df|([a,b]) \to 0$, i.e., $Df_n \to Df$ in **total variation norm of measures**. Each $Df_n$ is purely atomic. We show the limit $Df$ must be purely atomic.

Decompose any finite measure $\mu = \mu_a + \mu_c$ into its purely atomic and continuous (atom-free) parts. These are mutually singular, so for purely atomic $\mu_n$:

$$\mu_n - \mu = \underbrace{(\mu_n - \mu_a)}_{\text{atomic}} - \underbrace{\mu_c}_{\text{continuous}}.$$

Since the atomic and continuous parts are mutually singular,
$$|\mu_n - \mu| = |\mu_n - \mu_a| + |\mu_c|.$$

If $|\mu_n - \mu| \to 0$, then $|\mu_c| \to 0$, hence $\mu_c = 0$. So $\mu = Df$ is purely atomic, and $f$ is a saltus function. ✓

### Conclusion

Steps 1–3 together show:

$$\overline{\{\text{step functions}\}}^{\|\cdot\|_{BV}} = \{\text{saltus functions}\}.$$

## Answer

The closure of the space of step functions on a bounded interval with respect to the BV norm is the **space of saltus functions** (also called the **space of jump functions**): those functions of bounded variation whose distributional derivative is a purely atomic measure, equivalently, functions expressible as a (countable) sum of jumps with absolutely summable jump sizes.

